<Base title="The Compass Key Method" description="How Sharjeel Hashmat assesses a UAE property before you commit: price, returns, risk, exit, and a clear view on whether to buy.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Investment approach</p>
      <h1>The Compass Key Method</h1>
      <p class="lede">Clarity before commitment. Before you put capital into a UAE property, you should see the numbers, and hear an honest view on whether the deal deserves your money.</p>

      <h2>The five checks</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Price. What comparable units actually sold for, per Dubai Land Department records, not the asking price.</li>
        <li>Returns. Net yield after service charges, vacancy and management. For off-plan, the payment plan set against your cash flow.</li>
        <li>Risk. The developer's delivery record, the project's escrow status, and the supply arriving in the same area.</li>
        <li>Exit. Who buys this unit from you in five years, and at what realistic price.</li>
        <li>My view. Buy, negotiate, or pass. If the numbers don't work, I'll tell you not to buy.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How a Compass Key Brief is built</h2>
      <p>Send me any UAE property, by link or name. I run the five checks using sourced, dated figures and send you a short brief with the numbers and my recommendation. There is no obligation.</p>

      <h2>Why this approach</h2>
      <p>In UAE real estate since 2018, with a decade of experience across banking sales and senior management behind me, I read the cash flow before the brochure. That is the discipline behind every brief.</p>

      <a href="/contact">Get the numbers on a property</a>

      <p class="fine">Figures are sourced and dated. This is property analysis, not financial advice.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
